CAT = "cpaaudSampling"; SEG = "SMP"
Q = [
("In attribute sampling for a test of controls, which change increases the required sample size?",
 "A lower tolerable deviation rate", ["A higher tolerable deviation rate", "A lower expected deviation rate", "A higher acceptable overreliance risk"],
 "Sample size rises when the tolerable rate falls, the expected deviation rate rises, or the acceptable risk of overreliance falls."),
("In a test of controls, the risk of assessing control risk too low relates to the audit's:",
 "Effectiveness", ["Efficiency", "Timeliness", "Independence"],
 "Overreliance on a control that does not work lets misstatements go undetected, which hurts effectiveness. Underreliance only causes extra work."),
("In substantive sampling, the risk of incorrect rejection affects:",
 "Efficiency", ["Effectiveness", "Independence", "Materiality"],
 "Incorrect rejection leads the auditor to do more work on a fairly stated balance, which costs efficiency, not effectiveness."),
("An auditor tests 60 invoices for approval and finds 2 unapproved. The sample deviation rate is about:",
 "3.3%", ["1.7%", "2.0%", "6.0%"],
 "The sample deviation rate is 2 / 60 = 3.3%. The upper deviation limit adds an allowance for sampling risk to this rate."),
("After a test of controls, the upper deviation limit exceeds the tolerable deviation rate. The auditor should:",
 "Reduce reliance on the control", ["Accept the control as effective", "Raise the tolerable rate", "Ignore the deviations"],
 "If the upper limit exceeds the tolerable rate, the sample does not support the planned reliance, so control risk is assessed higher and substantive work increases."),
("For a large population, increasing the population size in attribute sampling has what effect on the sample size?",
 "Little effect", ["Raises it proportionally", "Lowers it sharply", "Doubles it"],
 "Once a population is large, its size has very little effect on the sample size needed for a given confidence and tolerable rate."),
("Monetary unit sampling is most suited to testing for:",
 "Overstatement of a balance", ["Understatement of a balance", "Zero-balance accounts", "Negative balances"],
 "MUS selects in proportion to recorded dollars, so large items are more likely chosen. It is weak at finding understatement and omits zero or negative balances."),
("With a tolerable misstatement of $90,000 and a reliability factor of 3.0, the monetary unit sampling interval is:",
 "$30,000", ["$27,000", "$45,000", "$270,000"],
 "The sampling interval equals tolerable misstatement divided by the reliability factor: $90,000 / 3.0 = $30,000."),
("In monetary unit sampling with a $25,000 interval, an account recorded at $40,000 is:",
 "Certain to be selected", ["Never selected", "Selected half the time", "Selected only if risky"],
 "Any item at or above the sampling interval is certain to be selected, since a selection point must fall within it."),
("In monetary unit sampling, an item recorded at $2,000 is audited at $1,500. With a $20,000 interval, the projected misstatement for it is:",
 "$5,000", ["$500", "$2,500", "$10,000"],
 "The tainting is $500 / $2,000 = 25%. Projected misstatement for an item below the interval is tainting times the interval: 25% x $20,000 = $5,000."),
("Ratio estimation is most useful when misstatements are:",
 "Proportional to book values", ["About equal in dollar amount", "Absent from the sample tested", "Only understatements of value"],
 "Ratio estimation projects using the ratio of audited to recorded value, so it fits when misstatement size moves with item size. Difference estimation fits roughly equal misstatements."),
("Sample items with book values totaling $160,000 are audited at $152,000. The population's book value is $2,400,000. Using ratio estimation, the estimated audited value is:",
 "$2,280,000", ["$2,392,000", "$2,240,000", "$2,520,000"],
 "The audited-to-book ratio is $152,000 / $160,000 = 0.95, and 0.95 x $2,400,000 = $2,280,000, an estimated overstatement of $120,000."),
("Stratifying a population before a variables sample mainly:",
 "Lowers the required sample size", ["Raises the sampling risk", "Removes all nonsampling risk", "Increases each stratum's variability"],
 "Stratification groups items of similar size, reducing variability within each stratum, which reduces the sample size needed for the same precision."),
("Applying the wrong procedure to a sample item, or missing a misstatement in an item tested, is an example of:",
 "Nonsampling risk", ["Sampling risk", "Allowance for sampling risk", "Tolerable misstatement"],
 "Nonsampling risk comes from causes other than sampling, such as poor procedures or human error. It is controlled through planning, supervision and quality management."),
("A selected sample item cannot be found, such as a voucher the client has lost. In a test of controls the auditor should:",
 "Treat it as a deviation", ["Replace it with another item", "Leave it out of the results", "Treat it as free of deviation"],
 "If the auditor cannot apply the procedure to a selected item and has no alternative, the item is treated as a deviation from the control."),
("Selecting every 40th invoice after a random start is called:",
 "Systematic selection", ["Haphazard selection", "Block selection", "Monetary unit selection"],
 "Systematic selection uses a fixed interval after a random start. Block selection takes a run of consecutive items, which seldom gives a representative sample."),
]
